The `while` loop in `ensureWatchers` can become an infinite async loop. When `setupWatchers` fails (e.g. the working directory is not a git repository, so `getGitDir` throws), the `catch {}` block swallows the error without setting any failure flag, and the `finally` block resets `watcherSetupInFlight = undefined` leaving `this.watchers` still empty. On the next iteration the condition `gitStateCallbacks.size > 0 && Object.keys(this.watchers).length === 0` is still true, so the loop retries indefinitely, scheduling new microtask frames forever and leaking memory.

Consider breaking out of the loop (or tracking a `watcherSetupFailed` flag) when `setupWatchers` completes without registering any watchers:
```ts
if (!this.watcherSetupInFlight) {
  this.watcherSetupInFlight = this.setupWatchers(this.watcherSetupGeneration);
}
await this.watcherSetupInFlight;
// If setup ran but produced no watchers, bail out instead of retrying.
if (Object.keys(this.watchers).length === 0) break;
```

`void this.configureBranchWatcher(gitDir, generation)` discards the promise returned by `configureBranchWatcher`. If `readHeadRef` encounters a non-ENOENT error and re-throws it (see the `catch` block in `readHeadRef`), the rejection propagates out of `configureBranchWatcher` as an unhandled promise rejection. The initial call site inside `setupWatchers` is protected by the outer `try/catch`, but this runtime callback is not.

Add a `.catch` to prevent unhandled rejections:
```ts
void this.configureBranchWatcher(gitDir, generation).catch(() => {
  // Reconfiguration is opportunistic; ignore errors here.
});
```
